v3.0.0 - unreleased

Main changes:
  -> A target can now be given a name, and the report is grouped by it as well as by language:
      "mezura frontend=./web backend=./api" answers how much of the project each part is, in one
      view and with one total. "mezura ./project tests=./project/tests" separates one part and keeps
      the rest under a row called "(unnamed)". Name nothing and the output is exactly what it was.
      A space separates one target from the next only once you have named one, so a path with a
      space in it still needs nothing but a comma next to it while you name nothing.
  -> Every piece of printed text can now be styled, not just the four colors of the overview.
      46 tokens are exposed, and each takes a color plus any of bold, italic, underline, dim and
      reverse, in any order. Run "--help style" for the full list.
  -> Comment lines are now counted and reported separately, with their own percentage.
  -> Language files are now updated automatically when you install a new version, so a correction to
      a language reaches you without you having to do anything. One you had changed yourself is
      replaced too, since an out of date language file counts wrongly, but your copy is kept under
      "data/replaced/<version>/" and the program names it, so you can carry your changes over.
      A language file of your own is never touched, and neither are your themes, your default
      configuration or "extension_priority.txt".
  -> 32 new languages: Objective-C, MATLAB, Lua, Perl, Prolog, Pascal, Delphi, OCaml, F#, Elm,
      Clojure, Lisp, Scheme, Julia, D, Carbon, COBOL, Fortran, Ada, VHDL, Erlang, GraphQL,
      PowerShell, Svelte, JSX, WebAssembly text, Assembly, and the shader languages HLSL, GLSL,
      WGSL, Metal and Slang.
  -> Objective-C takes ".m" and Perl takes ".pl", while ".h" stays with C Header. If you write
      MATLAB or Prolog instead, or want your headers counted as Objective-C, swap the line for it
      in "extension_priority.txt", or run with "--force-lang m=matlab".
  -> Two languages that claim the same extension no longer settle it silently. The winner is named
      in the new "extension_priority.txt" file of the data directory, and when nothing names it, the
      program says which language it gave the extension to and that it only did so alphabetically.
  -> New "--force-lang" command: "--force-lang m=matlab,pl=perl" decides which language an extension
      belongs to for this run, whether or not another language claims it. It overrides the priority
      file and, like every other command, can be saved in a configuration file.
  -> Extensions are now matched without regard to case, so a file named "MAIN.RS" counts as Rust.

(Breaking!)
  -> Color palettes are now called themes, and carry a whole look instead of four bar colors.
      "--color-palette" is now "--theme", "--show-palettes" is "--show-themes", "--tune-palettes"
      is "--theme-editor", and the files live in "data/themes" instead of "data/palettes".
      A theme file is a list of "token = value" lines and nothing else, the same tokens that
      "--style" takes. Palettes from older versions have to be rewritten in the new format.
  -> --colors is removed. The colors of the overview are now five ordinary style tokens,
      "language-1" to "language-4" and "language-others", so they are set like every other style
      and can carry bold or italic on the language name. A theme that names "language-4" and not
      "language-others" gets the same style for both, since the two never appear together.
  -> A configuration file declares "theme" instead of "color-palette", and can no longer declare
      "colors". Its "style" block stays, as the per project tweaks on top of the named theme.
  -> --no-visual and --no-keywords are removed, replaced by --hide. Use '--hide bar' and
      '--hide keywords'.
  -> The default order of the languages is now the number of lines, and ties are broken
      alphabetically. Use '--sort size' to get the old order back.
  -> The "details" section is now a table by default: one aligned row per language, with a
      percentage next to its files, lines, code and comments, and the keywords in their own block
      underneath. Use '--layout list' for one block per language instead, which is where the
      older shape went.
  -> The overview bar is drawn with the 'medium' character by default. Use '--bar-thickness slim'
      for the old bar, which is the one that renders on every terminal.
  -> "extra" no longer includes the comments: it is the blank lines plus the lines that carry
      no content.
  -> Lines that carry no content are recognised consistently now, whatever punctuation they are
      made of. The old rule covered only a few shapes, so a small number of lines move between
      "code" and "extra". '--braces-as-code' counts them all as code, as before.
  -> Log entries gained a "Comments" key. An entry written by an older version does not have one,
      so it is printed without an extra comparison instead of reporting a drop that never happened.
  -> Warnings and errors are written to the error output instead of the standard one, so that a
      redirected or piped run carries the report and nothing else. On screen nothing changes.
  -> mezura exits with code 1 when the run failed: an unrecognised command, a wrong value for one,
      a configuration, theme or language name that does not exist, a path that is not there, or a
      set of files where every single one failed to be parsed. Finding no code to count is not a
      failure and still exits 0, and so does a run where only some files failed.

New commands:
  -> --hide <list> : leaves parts of the output unprinted. Takes any of version, directory-info,
      parsing-info, keywords, overview, bar, progress and timing, separated by commas or spaces.
      Errors and warnings are never hidden, so hiding the parsing info still reports files that
      failed to be parsed.
  -> --style <token>=<value>,... : overrides the style of one or more categories of printed text,
      for a single run. Can also be stored in a configuration file and is kept by --save.
  -> --bar-thickness slim|medium|fat|low : chooses the character the overview bar is drawn with.
      'slim' is the default and the only ASCII one, so it is the fallback for terminals that
      cannot render box drawing characters.
  -> --layout table|boxed|list|matrix : chooses the shape of the "details" section. 'boxed' is the
      table inside a drawn frame, where each number shares a cell with its percentage. 'list' is the
      block of three rows per language that older versions printed. 'matrix' puts the languages down
      and the modules across, one number per cell, for comparing folders that are several answers to
      the same problem rather than several parts of one thing.
  -> --output text|json : 'json' replaces the whole printed report, status lines and overview
      included, with one JSON document, so a run can be read by another program. The counts go in
      as plain numbers of lines and bytes, with no separators, no KB or MB and no percentages, and
      warnings go to the error output so they never land inside the document as stray text. They are
      carried inside it under "warnings", each with a code, the subject it is about, the readable
      message, and whether it puts the counts or only the settings in doubt. A configuration file
      cannot declare it.
  -> --save-theme <name> : writes the way this run looks into a theme file, flattened into values,
      so it stands on its own and can be shared. Combined with --save, the configuration that is
      written points at that theme and carries no styles of its own.
  -> --number-separator comma|underscore|dot|none : chooses the character that groups the digits of
      every printed number. The character itself is accepted too, as in '--number-separator _'.
  -> --decimal-separator dot|comma : chooses the character that separates the decimals of the sizes,
      the percentages and the execution time. Together with the one above, '1.559.486' and '716,2'.
  -> --sort lines|files|code|size|name : chooses the order of the languages.
  -> --version : prints the version of the binary and the date it was released on.
  -> --restore : writes back every file that ships with mezura and is not in your data directory
      any more, the language files, the themes and the default configuration, and says which ones.
      It only creates what is missing: your own files and your edits to the shipped ones are never
      touched. A missing language file is the reason it exists, since that language just stops
      being counted without a word.
  -> --show-themes now previews a theme on a sample of the real details rows and a mock overview,
      instead of the overview alone, so the headings, labels, numbers, percentages and size figures
      it declares can be seen before it is picked. It also takes an optional thickness
      ('--show-themes fat'), so the preview bar matches what --bar-thickness would print.
  -> --top <n> : shows only the first n languages, and states underneath how many were hidden.
      The total keeps counting all of them, and the overview never shows more than n either.

Change in commands:
  -> The progress section marks an entry that was counted with different settings: "modified:"
      followed by the names of the ones that changed. The comparison is still shown, because the
      point is to say whether it can be trusted. Log entries also record '--no-gitignore' now, the
      one setting that changes the numbers and was not being written down.
  -> A size in bytes is printed as a whole number now, "900 Bytes" instead of "900.0 Bytes". Only a
      figure that was divided down to KBs, MBs or GBs has a decimal.
  -> 'details-language' is split in two: 'details-language-header' is the word "Language" over the
      first column of the two tables, 'details-language-name' is the name of a language wherever it
      appears. The header now reads like the other six instead of standing out on its own.
  -> The "Size" column header now reads like the other five instead of being darker than them, and
      the unit next to a size ("KBs", "MBs") has its own token, 'size-unit', which is the part that
      stays quiet. A theme that set 'total-size-label' to dim it was dimming the header too.
  -> Styles are applied in this order: built-in defaults, then the theme, then your configuration
      file, then --style. So a theme can carry a complete look, your own config can keep a few
      tweaks that survive switching themes, and --style wins over both.

Performance:
  -> Counting uses well under half the processor time it did, while counting more languages than
      before, and the counts themselves are unchanged. How much of that reaches the clock is decided
      by your disk: a large repository on a fast NVMe was already close to what the processor can
      do, while on a slower drive, or with antivirus scanning every file that is opened, the same
      repository is now counted in a little over half the time.
  -> mezura now uses many more threads for reading files, because a thread spends most of its time
      waiting for a file to open rather than working. This is worth nothing on a fast disk whose
      files were read recently, and up to twice the speed on a slow disk or on the first run after
      a reboot. '--threads' accepts up to 32 producers and 128 consumers now, instead of 8 and 30,
      so it is worth raising if your files live on a slow drive.

Bugfixes:
  -> Naming the same directory twice, once with a slash at the end, counted everything in it twice.
      "mezura ./src,./src/" now reports what "mezura ./src" reports.
  -> "--exclude-languages" now says so when it is given a name that is not a language, instead of
      silently excluding nothing. Asking for one with "--languages" always did.
  -> A directory that cannot be opened during the scan is now reported, instead of taking everything
      inside it out of the counts without a word. It is told the same way a file that fails to parse
      is: the count, and the paths themselves with "--show-faulty-files".
  -> The paths of faulty files are now written with forward slashes throughout, instead of mixing
      them with backslashes halfway through on Windows. This also applies to the JSON document.
  -> A scan that finds nothing after failing to open a directory now exits with a failure code,
      the same way a run where every file failed to parse always has: that zero is not a count of
      anything. Finding nothing in directories that could actually be read still exits 0.
  -> A path with "--" inside it, like the folder names some tools generate, is no longer cut at it
      and refused. A command begins only where "--" follows a space or starts the line, and the
      message-only commands answer only when written as whole words, so "--helpme" is an
      unrecognised command instead of the help, and a path containing "--version" does not print
      the version instead of counting.
  -> A configuration file that stops being readable partway, most often because it was saved in an
      encoding other than UTF-8, now stops the run and names the file and the line. It used to be
      applied up to the unreadable line and silently lose everything after it.
  -> A worker thread dying mid-run now stops the run with an error that carries the reason,
      instead of returning counts that are silently missing that thread's share, or in one
      arrangement never returning at all. If the operating system refuses some of the requested
      threads the run simply carries on with fewer, and only refusing every one of them is an error.
  -> A configuration file's "no-gitignore" and "search-in-dotted" now apply when the targets are
      being worked out, not only during the counting. A config that declared "no-gitignore" beside
      a glob of gitignored paths died with "doesn't exist anymore", while the same command line
      counted; the settings of the command line, the loaded configuration and the default one are
      now merged before any pattern is expanded, wherever the pattern came from.
  -> "--save" now writes a glob pattern as the pattern, made absolute, instead of the paths it
      happened to match at the moment of saving. Every load expands it fresh, so a configuration
      saved with "services/*" keeps meaning that as services come and go.
  -> "--search-in-dotted" no longer walks into ".git". It opens the dotted directories somebody made,
      like ".github", and leaves the one git keeps alone, at any depth. On this machine that is six
      thousand fewer files read for exactly the same counts. Naming ".git" as a target yourself still
      works, as writing out any path always has.
  -> A run that names its parts and finds nothing in them now lists them, empty, instead of leaving
      them out of the report and the document altogether.
  -> "--output json" now writes the whole document when every file fails to parse, faulty files and
      warnings included, where it used to write nothing at all. The exit code still says failure.
  -> A symbolic link, or a Windows junction, that the scan comes across is no longer followed, so
      whatever it points at is not counted a second time through it. The same goes for one that a
      glob pattern matched. Expect a smaller count on a project that links one of its own
      directories in. One that you name as a target yourself is still followed.
  -> CSS, HTML and SCSS are counted at all now. A language file that declares multiline comments but
      no keywords was rejected as badly formatted, so those three were dropped from every run with a
      one line warning and nothing else. Expect them to appear in stats where they were missing.
  -> The shipped language files describe their languages better. Python now declares triple quotes,
      so a docstring containing a single quote no longer swallows the lines after it: on the Linux
      kernel that alone moves 146 lines from "code" to "comments". JavaScript, TypeScript, TSX, Vue
      and Go declare their backtick strings, so a '//' inside a template literal or a raw string is
      no longer read as a comment. Java, Kotlin, Scala, Swift, C# and Elixir declare their triple
      quoted blocks. More extensions are recognised too: cc, cxx and c++ for C++, hh, hxx and h++
      for C++ headers, mjs, cjs and jsx for JavaScript, mts and cts for TypeScript, pyw and pyi for
      Python, zsh and ksh for Shell, csx for C#, and sc for Scala.
  -> If you are upgrading and not installing for the first time, delete the "languages" folder in
      your data directory: the improved language files above only reach a machine that does not
      already have them, and the folder is regenerated in full on the next run.
  -> A language file can declare any number of string and single line comment symbols now. Only the
      first one or two of each were ever used, and anything past them was ignored without a word.
  -> A string that stays open because every symbol that could close it is escaped is no longer cut
      short at a different string symbol on the same line. Languages with more than one string
      symbol, Python and Shell among them, count a few such lines differently than before.
  -> Deleting the languages or the configuration folder no longer costs you your themes and your
      default configuration. The program used to treat that as a fresh installation and write every
      shipped file over the top; now it only puts back what is missing.
  -> The percentage bar of the overview is now more accurate
  -> The dashed line above the total now matches the width of the total line.
  -> A theme file that cannot be fully read no longer stops the program. The lines that do not
      parse are reported and skipped, and the rest of the file still applies. A palette used to be
      rejected whole, and the run with it, without saying what was wrong.
  -> A section a config file declares that is not a command is now reported instead of being
      ignored in silence.
  -> A name that does not exist now says what it is close to, instead of only that it is wrong.
      This covers an unrecognised command, a theme, a configuration and the names given to
      '--languages'. When nothing is close, it points at the command that lists what exists.
  -> '--help' now has an entry of its own in the help, and '--help --help' prints it instead of
      printing nothing at all.

Other:
  -> Updated default styling. The 'list' layout was rebuilt too: each language is a block of three
      rows, with the file count and size above the name, the line breakdown beside it and the
      keywords below. It is about 50 columns narrower than the same block was before.
  -> Sizes of a gigabyte and above are reported in GBs.
  -> A language with many keywords wraps its keyword row, instead of making the details section
      wider than every other line in it.
  -> The test suite gained per-language parser fixtures that declare their own hand-verified counts,
      and a byte-exact comparison of the whole fixture corpus against a recorded result.
  -> mezura is now dual-licensed as MIT OR Apache-2.0, at your option.



